  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "A new bridge tore itself apart in a forty-mile wind. Washington State, November 1940. Four months old.",
        "visual_query": "puget sound suspension bridge",
        "visual_fallback": "suspension bridge"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The builders already called it Galloping Gertie. The deck rolled on ordinary days, and drivers crossed it for the ride.",
        "visual_query": "1940s american suspension bridge",
        "visual_fallback": "vintage bridge"
      },
      {
        "narration": "That morning the roll became a twist. One lane climbed while the other dropped, and by eleven the span was in the water. Forty miles an hour. How does that beat steel?",
        "visual_query": "steel suspension bridge cables wind",
        "visual_fallback": "bridge cables"
      },
      {
        "narration": "It is called aeroelastic flutter. The wind fed the bridge's own twisting, and the twisting pulled in more energy than it shed. Theodore von Karman sat on the board that proved it.",
        "visual_query": "wind tunnel engineering model 1940s",
        "visual_fallback": "wind tunnel"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Nobody died. A cocker spaniel named Tubby did. The wreck still lies a hundred and eighty feet down, now a protected reef.",
        "visual_query": "puget sound underwater wreck",
        "visual_fallback": "underwater steel"
      }
    ],
